          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Interesting idea. A few directions this could take in thos:\n\n**Trigger models:**\n- **Cron/schedule** — run a Claude Code agent at intervals (e.g., every hour, daily). Good for recurring tasks like code review, dependency audits, changelog summaries, test runs.\n- **File-watch** — trigger an agent when files change (fs.watch / chokidar). Useful for continuous linting, auto-docs, or test-on-save workflows.\n- **Event-driven** — trigger on git hooks (post-commit, post-merge), GitHub webhooks, or when another agent completes.\n- **Persistent loop** — an always-running agent that polls for work from a task queue.\n\n**Architecture-wise in thos:**\n- A new `server/scheduler.ts` that manages job definitions (stored in `.thos/jobs/`) and spawns `claude` CLI processes on trigger\n- Each spawned agent connects back to the WS server on `/claude/:agentId` like any other agent — the dashboard already handles visibility\n- Jobs config could look like:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"id\": \"nightly-review\",\n    \"schedule\": \"0 2 * * *\",\n    \"prompt\": \"Review all changes from today, file issues for anything concerning\",\n    \"cwd\": \"/path/to/repo\",\n    \"enabled\": true\n  }\n  ```\n- Dashboard gets a \"Jobs\" panel to create/edit/toggle/monitor scheduled agents\n- `node-cron` or similar for scheduling, child_process.spawn for launching CLI instances\n\n**Open questions:**\n- **Cost control** — autonomous agents can burn tokens fast. Need budget limits, max turns, or approval gates?\n- **Scope** — should cron agents have the same tool permissions as interactive ones, or more restricted?\n- **Chaining** — should one agent's output feed into another's prompt (pipelines)?\n- **Idempotency** — how to handle overlapping runs if a job takes longer than its interval?\n\nWant me to plan out an implementation, or are you still in the brainstorming phase?"
            }
          ],
          "stop_reason": null,
          "stop_sequence": null,
