              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\nSome use cases that come to mind:\n\n**Code maintenance**\n- Nightly dependency update checks — agent opens PRs for outdated packages\n- Dead code detection — periodic sweep for unused exports/functions\n- TODO/FIXME harvester — scans codebase, creates tasks in `.thos/tasks/`\n\n**Quality gates**\n- Post-commit reviewer — runs after every commit, flags issues before they pile up\n- Test coverage watchdog — monitors coverage drift, alerts when it drops\n- Type safety auditor — finds `any` creep, implicit casts, missing types\n\n**Documentation**\n- Changelog generator — summarizes git log into human-readable entries on a schedule\n- API docs sync — watches route files, regenerates docs when endpoints change\n- Architecture diagrams — periodically re-analyzes imports and generates dependency graphs\n\n**DevOps / ops**\n- Log analyzer — tails logs, surfaces anomalies or recurring errors\n- Performance regression detector — runs benchmarks nightly, compares against baseline\n- Environment drift checker — validates dev/staging/prod configs stay in sync\n\n**Agent-to-agent workflows**\n- Triage agent — watches incoming GitHub issues, labels and assigns them, spawns a worker agent for straightforward bugs\n- Research agent — given a topic, continuously gathers context across the codebase and summarizes findings for a human to review later\n- Refactor planner — identifies refactoring opportunities, queues them as tasks for interactive sessions\n\n**The interesting ones** are probably the compound workflows — an agent that notices something, creates a task, and another agent picks it up. That's where it stops being \"cron\" and starts being orchestration.\n\nWhat's pulling you toward this — a specific workflow you keep doing manually, or more the general concept?"
